# Eisen Chili
>With thanks to Ilana and her chili based on ['The Best Damn Chili' recipe](https://www.allrecipes.com/recipe/233613/best-damn-chili/)

## Ingredients

-   Olive oil
	-   Sunflower is OK too. The pan shouldn’t be getting super hot at any stage (though a high heat can be used to give an initial brown to the meat)
-   2 large yellow onions, finely chopped
    - 1-2 shallots optional
-   2 large red bell pepper, finely chopped
   -   Lots of garlic cloves, minced/shaved/pressed
-   900g lean (85/15 is fine) ground beef    
-   Worcestershire sauce
	-   Use 75% of what you’d use on a similar amount of meat for burgers
-   1 litre chicken stock
-   1 can of OK lager
    -   Simpler Times is my go to but Tecate is also good
    

-   1 (793g/28 ounce) can crushed San Marzano tomatoes
    

	-   If using normal San Marzanos, blitz the can with a stick blender
    

-   3 tablespoons tomato paste
    
-   4 parts chili powder
    
-   4 parts ground cumin
    
-   2 parts (chipotle) chili powder
    

	-   Have been intending to experiment with supplementing to 1 part home ground dried ancho chili powder and 1 part chipotle chili powder
    

-   2 parts smoked paprika
    
-   1 part ground coriander
    
-   Pinch of garlic powder
    
-   Pinch of cayenne to taste
    
-   40ml of a good hot sauce (not too heavy on the vinegar - you are looking for heat and deep chili flavors)
    
-   Salt to taste
    
-   (Optional) 3 drops Crab/Fish Oil
    
-   Dash of dried oregano
    
-   Ground black pepper fried in the initial oil
    
-   2 (453g/16oz) cans dark red kidney beans
    

  

## Instructions

  

1.  Use Southwestern Seasoning and Worcester sauce to season the ground beef
	- Cumin, chile powder, garlic powder, onion powder, coriander, black pepper, salt
    
2.  Oil and heat the dutch oven/large pot you are using
    
3.  Brown ground beef and break it into small pieces no larger than a quarter
    
4.  Remove ground beef
    
5.  Add onions, shallots, and garlic
    
6.  When the aromatics begin to caramelize a few minutes after becoming translucent, add the diced bell peppers
    
7.  Cook until aromatics are well browned and bell peppers are very soft
    
8.  Remove aromatics and bell peppers
    
9.  Add alcohol (strongest first if you are using multiple types) to dissolve the fond swiftly followed by the chicken stock
    
10.  Add San Marzano Can, tomato paste, and spices to mixture (if using)
    
11.  Bring to a high simmer briefly
    
12.  Reduce heat to a mild simmer and return the solid ingredients to the pot
    
13.  Cover the pot and simmer for a good while leaving a space for some of the water vapor to escape
    
14.  After at least an hour’s reduction as the ingredients begin to amalgamate, add any liquid seasonings (taco sauce, hot sauce, etc)
    
15.  One hour before removing add the cans of kidney beans
- Make sure to include aqua faba and you can rinse the cans and add that if there’s a lot of particulate stuck in the bottom
- (Optional) For a truly long cook, add the beans after three or so hours and then fully cover the chile and cook for another three to five hours with the lid fully covering the pot at a very low heat

16.  If serving immediately, add a small bit of coarse ground black and white pepper, mexican shredded cheese, and chives to the dish. Allow the chili to cool some before storing otherwise.
    
17.  I recommend having a carb with the chili to truly maximize the meal prep enjoyment. Sourdough bread, roti, and quinoa pasta are all decent options
  - Pasta is acceptable if you’re a heathen or pressed for time to get to the store
